  rules: 1-4 color-substitution rules, each defined by a local example triplet
  rule_example:
    structure: 3 collinear 4-adjacent cells source_pixel -> target_pixel -> anchor, same cardinal direction
    source_pixel and target_pixel are distinct non-bg non-teal colors
    anchor is the first cell of (or Chebyshev-distance<=1 to) a teal(8) component (the stencil)
    all (source_color, target_color) pairs are unique across rules
  stencil:
    teal(8) connected component (8-connectivity), 1-12 pixels
    one stencil per rule; anchor touches or belongs to it
    removed in output
  scattered_source_pixels:
    3-8 isolated pixels per rule, each matching that rule's source_color
    placed anywhere on the grid outside the rule-example region
    replaced in output by the stencil translated to center on the pixel, recolored to target_color
    stamps may go out of bounds (clipped) or overlap other stamps
    spurious rule avoidance: scattered src pixels of different src_colors must not form a 4-adjacent pair whose continuation direction reaches a teal cluster, or the solver discovers a false rule
  bystander_pixels:
    0-7 pixels whose color is not any source_color and not teal [observed]
    preserved unchanged in output
  output: same size; rule-example region (legend + teal) erased; each scattered source pixel stamped with recolored stencil; bystanders kept

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Choose grid size H x W (5-20 each), number of rules R (1-4)
    2. For each rule pick distinct (source_color, target_color) pairs from non-bg non-teal palette
    3. Design each teal stencil as a small 8-connected component (1-12 cells) with a designated anchor cell
    4. Build output first -- for each rule, pick 3-8 scattered positions for source pixels, stamp the stencil (recolored to target_color) centered on each; allow clipping and overlap
    5. Optionally add 0-7 bystander pixels of uninvolved colors
    6. Derive input from output -- erase all stamps, place scattered source pixels at their centers
    7. Place rule-example triplets -- for each rule, pick a cardinal direction, place source_pixel, target_pixel, anchor collinearly, attach teal stencil at anchor; ensure triplets don't collide with scattered pixels or each other
    8. Validate that the solver recovers the output from the constructed input

